#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4695a6d-1571-5cef-92fd-65a1424d3c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102229} {:answer/id #uuid "e764e75f-9dc1-5603-8d96-633391ac023a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102230} {:answer/id #uuid "20cb5b64-5df2-52a7-b755-66584d125b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102231} {:answer/id #uuid "23c5e819-0177-54dd-995a-3e2f383292b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102232} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce1b24b-c6d4-5305-bf5a-65da4c5b5093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20cb5b64-5df2-52a7-b755-66584d125b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102231} :answer-field/id #uuid "6954f89e-46cf-599c-b11b-0656f3bd3e9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbfa12ab-0e88-552b-be2d-570d6151a60f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160836" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\,[2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(2+2)-0 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305a627f-325f-5f3b-8d64-02abe4e04896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102261} {:answer/id #uuid "70656859-96a1-5e8a-8599-832418c1e00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534502} {:answer/id #uuid "887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{6}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534561} {:answer/id #uuid "8dea7678-d219-511f-b3fe-1db777950957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534573} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b4642d-b2a3-5966-82c9-11412eda5001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{6}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534561} :answer-field/id #uuid "223d1d79-de5b-5791-9ab7-fa8e90b4871f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54f23aef-c157-5234-9df4-3f529af50cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161302" :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x-y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933e24f-9535-56e6-8bd8-463ca459b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534404} {:answer/id #uuid "54ae8c91-054f-56c1-8736-87af40558e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534433} {:answer/id #uuid "5efef0b8-d72d-5a49-aec0-94be999fd7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534457} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c386ae-4bfc-514a-a465-86fbebbe089e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} :answer-field/id #uuid "a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161328" :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f64d453-abc2-52b8-9d32-f5007c10820a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{5}(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534329} {:answer/id #uuid "22931441-e80d-5d69-b5da-e45afb70211d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534340} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534353} {:answer/id #uuid "3809e723-7c0b-5a79-a57f-42ba07e98577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534373} {:answer/id #uuid "90b9897e-bd16-5166-a704-48996320daaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534353} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d007d0a-e619-522c-9617-4f4a41fec80b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e16a799c-84bb-5d3d-b669-6d70bdf08db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161350" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-3x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $3x\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-3x)=-3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-3x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{1+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{3x}^{3}\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(y-3x)^{3/2}]}_{y=3x}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-3x)^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\,{[\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})(3-3x)^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(0-{3}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8726e024-292d-59b4-8013-fd7bf0f67e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+e)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534558} {:answer/id #uuid "c5558c16-5207-5627-b751-3fcb910575e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((2+e)^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534707} {:answer/id #uuid "c66cb474-bc45-59be-8448-dccc9423f5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((2+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534710} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534726} {:answer/id #uuid "d59e1bf3-fe94-540a-b645-1d3ca9e31091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+e)^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534726} :answer-field/id #uuid "de37fba6-41ca-540b-a8fd-47ec845611d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f471502-6ac3-5895-874a-d7c88c5897e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161354" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $x\\le y\\le x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}+{0}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{x+1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}y]}_{y=x}^{y=x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}(1+{e}^{2x})^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
